	affiliations = {VIT Business School, VIT University, India; Indian Institute of Management Nagpur, MIHAN, Nagpur, 441108, India; Department of Management, University of Turin, Corso Unione Sovietica 218 Bis, Turin, 10134, Italy; Gnosis: Mediterranean Institute for Management Science, School of Business, University of Nicosia, Nicosia, 2417, Cyprus},
	abstract = {The present study integrates institutional theory, legitimacy theory, and the resource-based view to investigate how regulatory dynamics influence green innovation in Indian small and medium enterprises. We examine how regulatory pressure and regulatory trust affect small and medium enterprise innovation behavior using a mixed-method design that includes 14 in-depth interviews analysed using an inductive–deductive approach and a quantitative survey of 436 middle-level managers of Small and medium enterprises assessed using PLS-SEM. The results show that regulatory pressure is frequently viewed as a threat rather than an enabler of green innovation when organizational capabilities are limited. However, the study reveals a compelling finding: perceptions of fairness and dependability are fostered by clear, consistent, and transparent policies, and regulatory pressure significantly predicts trust in regulatory institutions. Perceived regulatory legitimacy shows a negative moderating effect on the relationship between green innovation and sustainable competitive advantage. When legitimacy is high, SMEs rely less on green innovation as legitimacy itself enhances competitiveness. Conversely, under low legitimacy, firms depend more on green innovation to build credibility, indicating a substitutive relationship between institutional legitimacy and innovation capabilities. By demonstrating the benefits of methodological triangulation and providing both rich contextual insights and broadly applicable theoretical categories, the study advances small and medium enterprise sustainability research. Policymakers can expedite sustainable industrial transitions and enable Small and medium enterprises to make concurrent economic and environmental advancements by matching regulatory strategies with small and medium enterprise needs. © 2026 The Authors},
