	abstract = {In recent times, the population of the entire world has been increasing at a continuous and irresistible pace like a rising tide. Eastern India has also aligned itself with this continuous journey of population growth. So, with this rapid population growth, the demand for energy consumption is also increasing. Most of this increased energy consumption comes from fossil fuels, such as natural gas, oil, coal, etc. But these resources are not endless; they are slowly on the verge of exhaustion. Therefore, it is crucial to focus on the development of renewable energy sources to responsibly meet future needs and protect the environment, even though eastern India has abundant hydropower. This paper identifies renewable energy sources other than hydropower in Eastern India and reveals a renewable energy source favorable for Eastern India using a multi-criteria decision-making (MCDM) approach. Seven criteria and five alternatives (solar energy, bioenergy, geothermal energy, ocean energy, and wind energy) related to the topic were taken to guide the study. The ideal option is selected from among the alternatives in the Intuitionistic Fuzzy environment using the Entropy and VIKOR methods. A new score and accuracy function has been developed to convert Intuitionistic Fuzzy Numbers (IFNs) (in discrete mode) into real numbers. Sensitivity and comparative analyses have been conducted in various real-world scenarios to validate the obtained results. © 2025 The Authors},
